| 3 | [5] | [5] | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 4 | [7] | [7] | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 5 | [8] | [8] | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 6 | [9] | [9] | 0 | 0 | 0 |

Input destinations: [3, 1, 5, 0, 6, 0, 4]. Sorted: [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9].
Occupied buckets: 6; maximum load: 2; sum of squared loads: 9.
\image(figures/bucket_sort_pipeline.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original worked example sorts to [1,2,3,5,7,8,9]. Empty buckets remain visible; each table row records the actual encounter order and stable insertion result.)
\alttext(The original seven integers scatter into seven ordered buckets. Bucket zero contains one and two, bucket two is empty, and the remaining occupied buckets contain one value each. Sorting and gathering yields the ascending sequence.)

The inclusive span is important: x-minus-minimum is nonnegative and strictly
less than the span, so every destination is between zero and n-minus-one.
The mapping is monotone. If b(x)<b(y), then x cannot exceed y. Sorting each
bucket therefore makes their concatenation globally sorted. Appending in input
order and moving only strictly greater predecessors preserves equal-key order;
the current API exposes integers, without satellite records to label duplicates.

The core widens before subtracting, fixing an overflow in the old expression
that cast x-minus-minimum only after doing the subtraction in int. To avoid
overflow even in the subsequent product, write n=qR+r for inclusive span R:

\[
\left\lfloor\frac{dn}{R}\right\rfloor
=dq+\left\lfloor\frac{dr}{R}\right\rfloor,\qquad d=x-\min.
\]

Here d and r are at most 2^32-minus-one, so their product fits uint64_t.
The result is exact integer arithmetic, without floating-point rounding or a
nonstandard wide integer in the library. The public mapping helper also accepts
bucket counts up to SIZE_MAX without allocating those buckets.

> Original occupancy experiment, with actual insertion work

Keep the original sample size 100, seed one, integer-uniform draws in [0,999],
and normal cluster with mean 500 and standard deviation four. The first two
clustered values are replaced by 0 and 999. Those outliers stretch the observed
range so the interior values land in a few neighbouring buckets. Input generation
now lives in datamunge; the report reads actual bucket records. Standard-library
distribution algorithms can differ across toolchains, so the displayed sample
is reproducible for this toolchain, without promising universal seeded outputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bucket_sort.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto samples=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_workloads(100,1);
  const auto uniform=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_trace(samples.uniform);
  const auto clustered=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_trace(samples.clustered);
  std::print("| sample | occupied buckets | largest load | sum of squared loads | key comparisons | shifts | final key stores |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [name,trace]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,const dm::algorithms::BucketSortTrace*>>{
      {"uniform",&uniform},{"clustered",&clustered}}) {
    auto expected=trace->input;std::sort(expected.begin(),expected.end());
    if (trace->output!=expected || dm::algorithms::bucket_sort(trace->input)!=expected) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,trace->nonempty_buckets,trace->max_occupancy,
        trace->occupancy_square_sum,trace->key_comparisons,trace->shifts,trace->placements);
  }
  std::vector<double> x,balanced,clumped;
  for (std::size_t b=0;b<uniform.buckets.size();++b) {
    x.push_back(b);balanced.push_back(uniform.buckets[b].input.size());clumped.push_back(clustered.buckets[b].input.size());
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(x,clumped,"clustered",dm::RGB{220,38,38})
      .bars(x,balanced,"uniform",dm::RGB{37,99,235})
      .title("Original 100-item experiment: actual bucket occupancy")
      .x_label("bucket index").y_label("items in bucket").size(1100,520)
      .save_svg("build/bucket_sort.svg");
  std::print("\nRanges: uniform [{},{}], clustered [{},{}]. The two endpoint outliers are retained.\n",
      uniform.minimum,uniform.maximum,clustered.minimum,clustered.maximum);
}
```
