\caption(The original uniform-versus-clustered occupancy plot is retained. Red bars are drawn first, then blue; the table supplies insertion counts independently of visual overlap. Concentration permits more insertion work but does not force it.)
\alttext(One hundred bucket positions show small blue uniform loads spread across the range and two tall red clustered loads near its middle, with single endpoint outliers.)

The cluster has repeated integer keys, and insertion sort never shifts equal
keys. A large bucket is therefore not automatically a worst case. The original
claim that the occupancy picture alone decides speed was too strong: order
and distinctness within each bucket also matter. We report logical operations,
not elapsed times or a platform performance ranking.

> The same occupancy, linear versus quadratic insertion work

Let m be the number of interior values and n=m+2. Put endpoints 0 and
M=(m+2)(m+1) around the distinct interior values 1 through m. Since
m*n < M+1, every interior value and zero map to bucket zero, while M maps
to bucket n-minus-one. The occupancies are always m+1 and one, with all
other buckets empty. Change only the order of the interior values:

\[
A_m=[0,1,\ldots,m,M],\qquad D_m=[0,m,m-1,\ldots,1,M].
\]

For A_m, each insertion stops at one false comparison: K=m and S=0.
For D_m, interior value at position i shifts i-minus-one earlier interior
values, then compares with zero and stops. Thus actual comparisons K and
shifts S are

\[
K(D_m)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}i=\frac{m(m+1)}2,\qquad
S(D_m)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}(i-1)=\frac{m(m-1)}2.
\]

Both inputs have the same squared-load sum (m+1)^2+1 and the same sorted
output. The native generator checks that M fits int before allocating.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bucket_sort_order_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> sizes,ascending_work,descending_work;
  std::print("| interior m | total n | largest load, both | squared-load sum, both | ascending comparisons | descending comparisons | descending shifts |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const std::size_t m:{8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024,2048}) {
    const auto ascending=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_trace(dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_collision_input(m,false));
    const auto descending=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_trace(dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_collision_input(m,true));
    if (ascending.output!=descending.output || ascending.shifts!=0 || ascending.key_comparisons!=m ||
        descending.key_comparisons!=m*(m+1)/2 || descending.shifts!=m*(m-1)/2 ||
        ascending.occupancy_square_sum!=descending.occupancy_square_sum) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",m,m+2,descending.max_occupancy,
        descending.occupancy_square_sum,ascending.key_comparisons,descending.key_comparisons,descending.shifts);
    sizes.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(m)));
    ascending_work.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(ascending.key_comparisons)));
    descending_work.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(descending.key_comparisons)));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(sizes,ascending_work,"ascending interior",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(sizes,ascending_work,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .lines(sizes,descending_work,"descending interior",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .points(sizes,descending_work,"",dm::RGB{220,38,38},4)
      .title("Identical bucket loads, different insertion work")
      .x_label("log2(interior count m)").y_label("log2(actual predecessor key comparisons)")
      .size(1100,520).save_svg("build/bucket_sort_order_work.svg");
}
```
