> Complexity: a second-moment bound and a sharper order-sensitive account

Let bucket b contain N_b values, and let r buckets be occupied. Each successful
insertion shift removes one strict inversion inside its bucket. Thus S is
exactly the sum of those inversion counts. Each insertion makes at most one
additional false comparison, and performs one final key store:

\[
S\le K\le S+n-r,\qquad P=n-r,\qquad
S\le\sum_b\frac{N_b(N_b-1)}2.
\]

Scattering, bucket allocation and gathering take linear work. With amortized
vector append costs, the nontrivial algorithm therefore takes Theta(n+S),
bounded above by O(n+sum_b N_b^2). Auxiliary storage is O(n); actual snapshots
also take O(n). Empty, singleton and all-equal inputs return through the
original shortcut, allocating no buckets and recording no fictional insertion
work. The all-equal case is linear because the range scan still visits n items.

For a *fixed* bucket partition and independent draws with bucket probabilities
p_b, expand each squared occupancy into single-item and pair indicators:

\[
\mathbb E\left[\sum_b N_b^2\right]
=n+n(n-1)\sum_b p_b^2.
\]

If n equally likely buckets are used, the sum is 2n-minus-one, giving expected
linear work even with insertion sort inside each bucket. This is the classical
probabilistic model, discussed by [Cormen and colleagues](https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262046305/introduction-to-algorithms/)
and [Bercea and Even](https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10499). It is an expectation over
inputs, not a random choice made by the sorter. Our integer implementation uses
the *observed* minimum and maximum, so the partition itself is sample-dependent;
the fixed-partition independence equation must not be applied literally to its
100-item table. That table is an executed experiment, not an expected-time proof.
Finite integer ranges can also create many equal keys in a bucket, reducing
shifts even when the squared-load bound is large.

> Boundary checks and the corrected full-range arithmetic

The full signed-range example would overflow the old int subtraction before
its cast. The checked mapping and shared sorter now handle both endpoints,
negative values and duplicate extremes. A trace shortcut means no buckets were
actually built; its zero occupancy counters are not a claim that the input has
no values. Counter overflow throws rather than silently corrupting evidence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <climits>
int main() {
  std::print("| input | sorted | shortcut | allocated buckets | comparisons | shifts |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& input:std::vector<std::vector<int>>{
      {},{42},{7,7,7,7},{INT_MAX,INT_MIN,0,-1,1,INT_MIN,INT_MAX}}) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_trace(input);
    auto expected=input;std::sort(expected.begin(),expected.end());
    if (t.output!=expected || dm::algorithms::bucket_sort(input)!=expected) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",input,t.output,t.shortcut,t.bucket_count,t.key_comparisons,t.shifts);
  }
  std::print("\nFull-range endpoint indices in seven buckets: {}, {}.\n",
      dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_index(INT_MIN,INT_MIN,INT_MAX,7),
      dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_index(INT_MAX,INT_MIN,INT_MAX,7));
  bool bad_count=false,bad_value=false,bad_size=false;
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_index(0,0,9,0); }
  catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {bad_count=true;}
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_index(10,0,9,10); }
  catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {bad_value=true;}
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::bucket_sort_collision_input(50000); }
  catch (const std::length_error&) {bad_size=true;}
  if (!bad_count || !bad_value || !bad_size) return 1;
  std::print("Invalid bucket count, out-of-range value, oversized generator rejected: {}, {}, {}.\n",
      bad_count,bad_value,bad_size);
}
