// result_end

The report uses fixed and generated inputs because there is no natural dataset
needed to understand this sorting mechanism. Native tests compare sorted output
with std::sort, bucket indices with an independent wide-integer rational oracle,
and shift/comparison counters with prefix-rank counts. Exhaustive short arrays,
full-range random inputs, huge allocation-free bucket counts and matched-load
families check the claims beyond the small displayed examples.

> References

- T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest and C. Stein (2022),
  [Introduction to Algorithms, fourth edition](https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262046305/introduction-to-algorithms/),
  section 8.4, bucket sort. This report uses an observed inclusive integer range
  rather than the textbook fixed unit interval.
- I. O. Bercea and G. Even (2020), [Upper Tail Analysis of Bucket Sort and Random
  Tries](https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10499), doi:10.48550/arXiv.2002.10499.
  This primary analysis distinguishes inner sorting algorithms and their
  probabilistic costs; the report derives its elementary second-moment bound.
- P. E. Black (2019), [bucket sort](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/bucketsort.html),
  NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures, definition and variants.
